narrative:
Apothecary Nella Vey corrects your use of the word 'contagion' before answering. Her own cough is hidden behind a sleeve.

@Nella: It is recurrent, not new. The grammar matters. So does the ruin cluster where it always begins.

She gives you directions with the precision of someone afraid to be asked to come along.

Rumors outrun wagons. Healer Yssa Coombe has turned a stable into a clinic, while Quarantine-Warden Bettina Grask counts bottles as if numbers might multiply under pressure. Fen, the gravedigger's boy, waits nearby with mud on his boots.

@Yssa: I need hands, clean water, and people who do not lie about being ill. I will accept two.
@Fen: My mum says don't sell your coat. She sold hers. That was before winter.

Elder Pella dies before the bells finish their first warning. The clinic fills in a day. Hendrik's cough has become blood on a cloth he tries to hide. Yssa does not soften the truth.

@Yssa: We have one prepared dose that might carry him through. There will be other people who need it.

Outside, Corvin Ashby tells a frightened crowd that the sickness has chosen them for a reason.

At dawn, the fever simply stops. Nella Vey is more frightened by this than she was by the bodies.

@Nella: Diseases do not forgive. They finish, or they change. This did neither.

No one has an answer worth carrying home.

The clinic empties out in the slow, undramatic way rooms empty after something enormous has happened in them. Hendrik is propped up on borrowed pillows, insisting far too loudly that he intends to walk home unassisted. Yssa Coombe has already told him twice that intending is not the same as doing.

@Hendrik: Don't fuss. Weather gets into old lungs. Always has. It let go of me same as it always does.

His hands, when he reaches for his tea, are steadier than his voice.

narrativeVariants.npc.old_hendrik.status == 'dead':
The clinic empties out in the slow, undramatic way rooms empty after something enormous has happened in them. Hendrik's chair by the stove has already been taken by someone else who needed the warmth more urgently than grief did. Someone has folded his coat and set it on the seat anyway, like the room itself couldn't quite agree to the arrangement.

Yssa found his tools sorted and labeled in a small chest under his cot, done sometime before the fever took the words to explain why. There's a note tucked in with them, in handwriting that gets worse toward the bottom of the page.

@Hendrik's note: If you're reading this I ran out of time to say it properly, so here it is plainly: thank you for treating an old man's stories like they were still worth telling. Take the tools. Something in this world should keep working because of me.

narrative:
It takes most of the afternoon. Hendrik's hands shake more than they used to, so the lesson is slower than either of you expected - re-setting a blade's edge the old way, reading a whetstone's grain instead of trusting its label, the particular patience of a craft that punishes shortcuts.

@Hendrik: There. Now if I go before I mean to, something of me keeps working instead of just keeps being missed. That's the trade I'd make, if anyone asked me, which nobody does.

He presses his own worn toolkit into your hands before you can argue about it.

@Hendrik: Don't thank me. Use it badly a few times first. That's how it becomes yours.

@You: The lesson can wait. You can't teach anything properly this tired.

Hendrik looks almost disappointed, then almost relieved, then settles on pretending he wasn't going to argue anyway.

@Hendrik: Suit yourself. Come back when I've got the strength to complain about your technique. I intend to have opinions about it.

He's asleep again before you're fully out the door.

Mira's handwriting has gotten faster since the last letter, like she's stopped worrying about how it looks.

@Mira: Tovin's grown two inches and lost a tooth, in case you were tracking either. The coop held through the worst of winter. But Old Rennick's holding a debt over Farmer Dessa's head - foreclosure by spring unless someone finds coin she doesn't have, and she's too proud to ask the coop directly, so I am asking on her behalf, badly, in writing, because I couldn't say this to your face without it sounding like begging.

@Mira: I'm not asking you to fix everything. I'm asking because you're the only person I know who might actually be positioned to.

The letter ends the way her letters always end now - no flourish, just her name, like she still isn't sure how much of herself to put on a page that might not be read the way it was meant.
